{"files":[{"additions":38,"blob_url":"https://github.com/wstein/flatbars/blob/27cda443965a9225ba0e516ed3e6a1bd7d6d7bfe/tutorials%2Fsrc%2Fpages%2Fstart.astro","changes":70,"contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wstein/flatbars/contents/tutorials%2Fsrc%2Fpages%2Fstart.astro?ref=27cda443965a9225ba0e516ed3e6a1bd7d6d7bfe","deletions":32,"filename":"tutorials/src/pages/start.astro","patch":"@@ -2,15 +2,16 @@\n // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n //\n // \"Getting started\" — the on-ramp (NOT a fifth reference). It gets you rendering\n-// in one example, helps you pick a dialect by the job, teaches the one discipline\n-// that keeps templates simple (shape data in the host), names the one real trap\n-// (truthiness) and how to disarm it, then shows the moment MaxBars earns its keep.\n+// in MaxBars (the promoted flagship surface — the only one with the full\n+// expressiveness), helps you pick a smaller dialect when a constraint calls for it,\n+// teaches the one discipline that keeps templates simple (shape data in the host),\n+// and names the one real trap (truthiness) and how to disarm it.\n //\n // Per the team consensus, this page forks NO normative prose (theory lives in\n // concepts.adoc, linked) and adds NO example corpus: every runnable beat reuses an\n // already-gated example object, shown through the same mechanism its home gate\n // validates — so the page inherits coverage:\n-//   • mustache.hello / mustache.truthyZero / fullbars.ifBlock / maxbars.comparison\n+//   • maxbars.intro / mustache.truthyZero / fullbars.ifBlock / maxbars.comparison\n //       → plain render, gated by scripts/check-tutorial-links.mjs\n //   • analyse.zero  → the cross-engine point; its analyze() finding is gated by\n //       scripts/check-tutorial-tooling.mjs (here it is a plain FullBars render that\n@@ -42,23 +43,23 @@ const groups = [\n   {\n     label: \"References\",\n     items: [\n-      { href: \"/minbars\", label: \"MinBars (Mustache)\" },\n+      { href: \"/maxbars\", label: \"MaxBars (flagship)\" },\n       { href: \"/transform\", label: \"Data shaping\" },\n       { href: \"/analyse\", label: \"Truthiness\" },\n     ],\n   },\n   {\n     label: \"Play\",\n-    items: [{ href: \"/lab/index.html?engine=minbars\", label: \"FlatBars Lab ↗\" }],\n+    items: [{ href: \"/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars\", label: \"FlatBars Lab ↗\" }],\n   },\n ];\n ---\n \n <Reference\n   title=\"Getting started\"\n-  subtitle=\"Render your first template, pick the dialect that fits the job, and keep templates simple by shaping data in the host — plus the one truthiness trap and how to disarm it.\"\n+  subtitle=\"Render your first template in MaxBars — the flagship surface with the full expressiveness — then learn the one habit that keeps templates simple, the one truthiness trap, and when a smaller dialect fits the job.\"\n   groups={groups}\n-  dialect=\"minbars\"\n+  dialect=\"maxbars\"\n   spec=\"concepts\"\n >\n   <style>\n@@ -80,37 +81,41 @@ const groups = [\n     <h2>Your first template</h2>\n     <p>\n       A template is just text with <code>{t(\"{{ }}\")}</code> holes that data fills in.\n-      Edit the template or the data below and watch it render — through the same engine\n-      the FlatBars Lab ships.\n+      This is <b>MaxBars</b>, the flagship surface — here a pipe (<code>| capitalize</code>)\n+      and a helper (<code>count</code>) already showing the full expressiveness. Edit the\n+      template or the data below and watch it render — through the same engine the FlatBars\n+      Lab ships.\n     </p>\n-    <OpenInLab client:visible engine=\"minbars\" template={mustache.hello.template} data={mustache.hello.data} />\n-    <p>That's the whole loop: <em>template + data → text</em>. Now let's pick the right tool.</p>\n+    <OpenInLab client:visible engine=\"maxbars\" template={maxbars.intro.template} data={maxbars.intro.data} />\n+    <p>That's the whole loop: <em>template + data → text</em>. MaxBars is what we recommend by default; next, when you'd reach for a smaller surface instead.</p>\n   </section>\n \n   <!-- ─────────────────────────── 2 · Which dialect? ───────────────────────── -->\n   <section id=\"choose\">\n     <h2>Which dialect?</h2>\n     <p>\n       FlatBars isn't one language — it's a meaning-free core with four surfaces built on\n-      it. They aren't tiers of quality; pick by your <b>constraint</b>, not by prestige.\n+      it. <b>MaxBars is the flagship: the full expressiveness, and what we recommend by\n+      default.</b> Reach for a smaller surface only when a specific <b>constraint</b> calls\n+      for it — they aren't tiers of quality, they trade features for guarantees.\n     </p>\n     <table class=\"choose\">\n       <thead><tr><th>Your job</th><th>Use</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n       <tbody>\n         <tr>\n-          <td>Logic-less email / CMS snippets; Mustache parity</td>\n-          <td><a href=\"/minbars\">MinBars</a></td>\n-          <td>the smallest surface — one sitting to learn it all; 184/184 of the Mustache spec</td>\n+          <td>App views with real expressions (<code>x &gt; 3</code>, pipes, loop vars) — the default</td>\n+          <td><a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a></td>\n+          <td>the flagship — infix operators, pipes, bare loop variables; the only surface with the full expressiveness</td>\n         </tr>\n         <tr>\n           <td>Importing existing Handlebars templates</td>\n           <td><a href=\"/fullbars\">FullBars</a></td>\n           <td>Handlebars-faithful (49/50 vs the real engine); your team already knows it</td>\n         </tr>\n         <tr>\n-          <td>App views with real expressions (<code>x &gt; 3</code>, pipes, loop vars)</td>\n-          <td><a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a></td>\n-          <td>the flagship surface — infix operators, pipes, bare loop variables</td>\n+          <td>Logic-less email / CMS snippets; Mustache parity</td>\n+          <td><a href=\"/minbars\">MinBars</a></td>\n+          <td>the smallest surface — one sitting to learn it all; 184/184 of the Mustache spec</td>\n         </tr>\n         <tr>\n           <td>Building your own engine on the core</td>\n@@ -120,10 +125,9 @@ const groups = [\n       </tbody>\n     </table>\n     <p class=\"tip\">\n-      It's a superset ladder — RawBars ⊂ FullBars ⊂ MaxBars — plus logic-less MinBars\n-      off to the side. The <a href=\"/minbars\">MinBars reference</a> is the recommended\n-      place to <em>learn the language</em>; the rest of this page is the recommended\n-      first ten minutes.\n+      It's a superset ladder — RawBars ⊂ FullBars ⊂ MaxBars — plus logic-less MinBars off\n+      to the side. <a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a> gives you everything the others do and\n+      more, so it's the recommended default; this page's first ten minutes use it.\n     </p>\n   </section>\n \n@@ -186,10 +190,11 @@ const groups = [\n \n   <!-- ─────────────────────────── 5 · Outgrow {{#if}} ──────────────────────── -->\n   <section id=\"outgrow\">\n-    <h2>When <code>{t(\"{{#if}}\")}</code> isn't enough → MaxBars</h2>\n+    <h2>Why the flagship: real expressions</h2>\n     <p>\n-      Start where your team is. In FullBars a condition is a Handlebars\n-      <b>subexpression</b> — a helper application, <code>{t(\"(gte score 90)\")}</code>:\n+      Here's the expressiveness in one comparison. Coming from Handlebars, a condition is a\n+      <b>subexpression</b> — a helper application, <code>{t(\"(gte score 90)\")}</code>\n+      (FullBars, on the left):\n     </p>\n     <div class=\"pair\">\n       <div>\n@@ -202,20 +207,21 @@ const groups = [\n       </div>\n     </div>\n     <p>\n-      The day <code>{t(\"(gte score 90)\")}</code> wants to read <code>score &gt;= 90</code>\n-      — with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, pipes (<code>value | helper</code>), and bare loop\n-      variables — that's <a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a>. It's the same engine and prelude\n-      as FullBars, just an expression surface on top; you don't restart, you grow into it.\n+      <a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a> reads it as <code>score &gt;= 90</code> — with\n+      <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, pipes (<code>value | helper</code>), and bare loop variables.\n+      That's the full expressiveness, and why it's the recommended default. It's the same\n+      engine and prelude as FullBars, just an expression surface on top — so if you do start\n+      on a smaller dialect, you grow into MaxBars without restarting.\n     </p>\n   </section>\n \n   <!-- ─────────────────────────── 6 · Next ─────────────────────────────────── -->\n   <section id=\"next\">\n     <h2>Where to go next</h2>\n     <ul>\n-      <li><a href=\"/minbars\">MinBars</a> — learn the whole logic-less language, tag by tag.</li>\n-      <li><a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a> — the expressive flagship: infix, pipes, loop variables.</li>\n+      <li><a href=\"/maxbars\">MaxBars</a> — the expressive flagship, tag by tag: infix, pipes, loop variables. <b>Start here.</b></li>\n       <li><a href=\"/transform\">Data shaping</a> — the JSONata habit from section 3, in depth.</li>\n+      <li><a href=\"/minbars\">MinBars</a> — the whole logic-less language, if you need Mustache parity.</li>\n       <li><a href=\"/lint\">Linting &amp; migration</a> — keep templates canonical, import Handlebars.</li>\n     </ul>\n   </section>","raw_url":"https://github.com/wstein/flatbars/raw/27cda443965a9225ba0e516ed3e6a1bd7d6d7bfe/tutorials%2Fsrc%2Fpages%2Fstart.astro","sha":"0b7ac4d55c690b2fb7cd2e704480f62cf01ef043","status":"modified"}]}